The linear layer (Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, 2016) was used to convert the final output to the probability of whether or not an antimicrobial peptide, which in this study consists of three fully connected layers: (8)\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{wasysym}
\usepackage{amsfonts}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{amsbsy}
\usepackage{upgreek}
\usepackage{mathrsfs}
\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
\begin{document}
\begin{eqnarray*}{y}_{i}=\sigma \left( w{x}_{i}+b \right) \end{eqnarray*}\end{document}yi=σwxi+b
where σ denotes the activation function, log_softmax (Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, 2016), used in this study, was used to convert the output of the fully connected layer. yi denotes the final output. w denotes the trainable weights, b denotes the bias unit, and xi denotes the output of the last fully connected layer.

